session that we have

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:22.150
for our monthly Colloquium
Series on the Inference project.

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:24.880
So as you all know yesterday

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:28.873
we had a wonderful talk by
professor Jim Weatherall,

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:33.930
of University of California, Irvine

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:35.427
and the title of his talk was,

00:00:35.427 --> 00:00:39.170
"Why do Groups Polarize
over Matters of Fact?

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:42.524
What Models Can and Cannot Tell Us?"

00:00:42.524 --> 00:00:47.524
So, we started with, he
started with telling us

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:49.600
about these four different approaches

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.670
and he outlined the
limits and the advantages

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:55.970
of each of these modeling approaches

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:59.780
to look at highly
polarized groups of people.

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:02.650
And this kind of agent modeling,

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:04.200
it give us a sort of an introduction

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.240
to some of these agent
modeling techniques,

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:10.170
which scholars in the social
sciences have often used

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:13.570
in lieu of the kinds of
simulations that physicists,

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:18.020
astronomers and people in
climate modeling and other fields

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:19.917
use sort of simple sort
of numerical models.

00:01:19.917 --> 00:01:23.440
So it's a new kinds of simulations

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:27.100
that help us examine social behavior.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:31.760
So, today it is my pleasure to introduce

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.070
our discussant professor, Kevin Dorst,

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:38.210
who is on the faculty at
the department of philosophy

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:40.890
at the University of Pittsburgh,

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:43.860
where he is an assistant professor.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:47.120
And he received his PhD from MIT,

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.150
spent a year as a Junior Research Fellow

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:54.870
at Magdalen College in Oxford
before starting this position.

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:59.380
And all of his research
projects have centered

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:03.180
around one kind of meta big question

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:07.780
which is "What Should We
Think About How People Think?

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:11.600
And typically psychology is a discipline

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:15.140
that is used to answer this question.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:18.990
And psychology informs us
that people's thinking that

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:22.530
we are actually very irrational as people,

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:27.210
unlike the premise in often
a lot of economic modeling

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:30.170
where we are supposed to
make rational choices.

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:32.690
But his work has shown

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:35.660
that these sort of
irrationalist interpretations

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:38.470
of data are actually questionable

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:42.610
and they rely on very simple models

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:44.350
of what rational people would think

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:46.850
and also these two
contrasts of modeling people

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:48.910
as either rational or irrational.

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:53.400
And his work has tried to
bring much more philosophically

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:56.570
sophisticated models of rationality,

00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:58.900
to bear on the
interpretation of these types

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.510
of empirical results reclaimed

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:05.890
in order to help assess
the degree of irrationality

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:09.373
and how, assess how
irrational we really are.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:13.730
And so this is sort of
his big picture view

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:18.610
and it's connection to
polarization in politics

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:22.837
is something that he
explores in his blog post,

00:03:22.837 --> 00:03:24.660
"A Plea for Political Empathy."

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:27.730
So I urge you all to go
and take a look at it.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:30.500
And for an extended
argument that polarization

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:34.310
has its roots in rational causes,

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:36.500
see the series, "Reasonably Polarized:

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:39.530
why politics is more
rational than you think."

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:42.330
So I urge you to go take
a look at his blog feed

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:45.100
which is really really exciting.

