00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:46.720
So starting with a sort of summary of what

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:48.810
we talked about yesterday so,

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:50.700
Jim introduced three different models

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:54.280
that lead to polarization
from some possible

00:06:55.497 --> 00:06:59.270
of cause is the first is trust dynamics.

00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:03.300
So, we have a bunch of little agents

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:05.430
a network that have some lines

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:07.280
of communication between each other.

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:11.490
They have to decide which
of two options to perform

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:13.680
option B or option A.

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:16.110
Option A they know it's expected value.

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:19.880
Option B they're unsure of
how good an option A is.

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:23.220
The standard example is,
which treatments I prescribed

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:25.143
to my patients doctors might wonder.

00:07:26.230 --> 00:07:30.290
And if you're interested
in some, examples of this

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:35.060
Jim and Cailin in their book,
and I think also in the paper

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:40.040
this is based on detailed
case study of this.

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:44.540
What's called the Lyme wars,
the debate over whether

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:49.070
chronic Lyme disease is
basically it's a thing,

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:52.830
whether chronic Lyme
disease, whether Lyme disease

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:54.600
can that has been treated with antibiotics

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:57.490
can sometimes linger and
therefore get further benefits

00:07:57.490 --> 00:07:58.590
from further antibiotic treatment

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:01.170
is a interesting polarized, scientific

00:08:04.670 --> 00:08:07.670
arguably scientific community
polarize on that question.

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:09.240
And so in that case you might think,

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:11.510
well some doctors are
deciding whether to describe

00:08:11.510 --> 00:08:14.360
do I prescribe antibiotics
or something else

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:18.780
for this patient who may or
may not have chronic Lyme.

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:21.050
And so that's the sort of cases I think

00:08:21.050 --> 00:08:22.700
where this model is most at home.

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:26.020
And the way this works is that

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:30.790
they learn both from doing
their own tests or actions.

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:32.640
They just, they take some options A or B

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:33.647
and just learn how good they are

00:08:33.647 --> 00:08:36.230
in trying to learn whether it be as better

00:08:36.230 --> 00:08:37.410
but they also learn from their neighbors.

00:08:37.410 --> 00:08:39.000
Their neighbors share information

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:40.450
about how their own tests went.

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:44.780
And the key twist is
that how much they trust

00:08:44.780 --> 00:08:46.540
their neighbors depends on

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:49.070
how close their neighbors
opinions are to their own.

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:51.290
In particular, how confident
in their neighbors are

00:08:51.290 --> 00:08:53.610
that option B is better
than option A is a proxy

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:55.470
for how much they trust them.

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:59.230
And if they trust them
less, they'll update less

00:09:00.452 --> 00:09:03.610
or basically treat their
information as less reliable

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:07.690
than if they trust them more
if their credences are closer.

00:09:07.690 --> 00:09:09.410
And the upshot is basically that

00:09:09.410 --> 00:09:11.650
when trust diminishes rapidly enough,

00:09:11.650 --> 00:09:14.850
based on how far peoples far
apart people's opinions are

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:15.990
we often get polarization.

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:19.440
One group ends up pretty
confident that A is better.

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:21.760
They stop trusting the
people who are using B

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:23.000
and they're since they're doing A,

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:25.680
they don't learn anything
else about how good B is.

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:27.550
While another group ends
up certain that B is better

00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:29.330
or arbitrarily confident that B is better

00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:30.330
because they keep on doing B

00:09:30.330 --> 00:09:33.583
and keep on eventually getting
evidence that it's better.

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:37.970
So that was the first trust dynamic model.

00:09:37.970 --> 00:09:40.230
And so we ended up with a polarized group

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:41.820
because basically the sides don't listen

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:42.920
to each other anymore.

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:46.810
The second model was a
conformity based model.

00:09:46.810 --> 00:09:49.170
So again, same structure
we got agent setting

00:09:49.170 --> 00:09:50.850
which to do A or B they're on a network.

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:52.640
They can learn from their neighbors

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:55.123
that they share a link
with on that network.

00:09:56.540 --> 00:09:58.660
But now, instead of having variable trust

00:09:58.660 --> 00:10:01.170
based on the degrees of confidence,

00:10:01.170 --> 00:10:03.300
agents feel some pressure.

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:05.210
You might think of this as
a social pressure to conform

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:07.270
to their neighbors the people
that they share links with,

00:10:07.270 --> 00:10:10.100
the people they get information
from and can talk to

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:13.460
or maybe trust if any how
we interpret those links.

00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:17.530
Those are the people that
think it's some extra utility

00:10:17.530 --> 00:10:19.620
or value from doing what
those people are doing.

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:24.590
If everyone in my social circle
is a Lyme literate doctor

00:10:24.590 --> 00:10:27.080
as they say, and prescribes antibiotics

00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:30.940
and response to symptoms
of apparent chronic Lyme

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:32.873
then I feel pressure to do so myself.

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:36.990
Some and so I might be
inclined to do so even

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:41.430
if I don't believe that
chronic Lyme is susceptible

00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:42.630
to antibiotic treatment.

00:10:44.734 --> 00:10:47.000
And there was the basic
result here is that often

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:49.260
this doesn't lead to polarization

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:51.480
but when the network has
a particular structure

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:53.480
in particular what has a click structure

00:10:54.530 --> 00:10:56.240
it can lead to polarization.

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:58.540
So click structure is
where you have these two

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:02.850
or many different groups
that are all sort of

00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:06.700
well interconnected, but
they are loosely connected

00:11:06.700 --> 00:11:09.183
to another group sort of like that.

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:14.370
Hopefully you remember that

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:16.160
how those look and the idea is.

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:19.540
Well, sometimes when you
have the sort of structure

00:11:21.070 --> 00:11:23.587
you can have a clique of
people that are all doing A.

00:11:23.587 --> 00:11:26.220
And so they're not learning
about how good B is

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:28.320
and maybe there's one person on the end

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:29.650
who says all these people are doing A.

00:11:29.650 --> 00:11:31.350
And there's one person on the A to

00:11:32.660 --> 00:11:34.690
this is like, does B, this person learns

00:11:34.690 --> 00:11:37.070
from seeing these people
that B is actually better.

00:11:37.070 --> 00:11:38.160
But they don't switch their action

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:40.120
because they feel pressure
to conform to their group.

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:41.610
And because they don't switch

00:11:41.610 --> 00:11:45.150
then these people who don't
have direct connections

00:11:45.150 --> 00:11:47.710
with other groups, never
learned that B is better.

00:11:47.710 --> 00:11:50.040
And so everyone still set does A.

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:52.750
So the idea is basically the sparse

00:11:52.750 --> 00:11:54.700
informational connections between clicks

00:11:54.700 --> 00:11:57.510
prevent the information suggesting

00:11:57.510 --> 00:11:58.670
that B is better from breaking

00:11:58.670 --> 00:12:01.330
into this certain part of the network.

00:12:01.330 --> 00:12:05.320
So that's where we get
polarization and action

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:07.660
and also beliefs sometimes many
of these people will believe

00:12:07.660 --> 00:12:10.357
that A is better, or at least they can.

00:12:12.740 --> 00:12:17.330
The third model then no longer
had a network structure.

00:12:17.330 --> 00:12:22.330
It was based on reporting
practices of of media outlets

00:12:22.770 --> 00:12:24.790
and how those interact or might interact

00:12:24.790 --> 00:12:28.430
with people's response to information

00:12:28.430 --> 00:12:31.580
and tendency perhaps to
confirm their beliefs.

00:12:31.580 --> 00:12:33.880
So the model in this case

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:38.130
we sort of model journalists
are observing the world.

00:12:38.130 --> 00:12:39.360
They're observing earthquakes,

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:42.250
they're observing economic consequences

00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:47.250
or what have you said, some regular news

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:50.580
that they can report on
that is in some sense

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:52.110
represent one numerical scale in terms

00:12:52.110 --> 00:12:54.840
of how severe or how good or bad they are.

00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:56.970
And they have different
reporting practices.

00:12:56.970 --> 00:12:58.200
They have to select what to report

00:12:58.200 --> 00:13:00.650
so they can use hyperbole,

00:13:00.650 --> 00:13:03.080
they can make things sound
more extreme than they are.

00:13:03.080 --> 00:13:05.030
They can use extreme
reporting which is they just

00:13:05.030 --> 00:13:06.430
they only report on extreme events

00:13:06.430 --> 00:13:11.120
ones very far from some
expected or neutral baseline,

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:13.410
or they do fair reporting
where they sort of like

00:13:13.410 --> 00:13:14.490
both sides journalism.

00:13:14.490 --> 00:13:17.100
Where they have whenever
they get their time

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:20.120
to a bit of information pointing one way,

00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:21.030
they also give their time

00:13:21.030 --> 00:13:23.203
to a bit of information
pointing the other.

00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:27.840
If we take those three different types

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:32.130
of journalistic methods and then we add

00:13:32.130 --> 00:13:34.440
that the agents watching
them have confirmation bias

00:13:34.440 --> 00:13:37.680
of a particular form, then
we can get polarization.

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:41.160
So in particular, the model
of confirmation bias is just

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:44.740
that people sometimes ignore journal,

00:13:44.740 --> 00:13:47.250
the reports of journalists

00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:50.800
and how likely they are to
ignore report is proportional

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:52.940
to how surprising they find that report.

00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:57.770
So they, at a given time they
have certain beliefs about

00:14:01.530 --> 00:14:05.550
basically how likely the
event that the economy

00:14:05.550 --> 00:14:07.600
is to be doing well or badly or whatever.

00:14:09.370 --> 00:14:12.320
And how likely they are to
ignore report to scales with

00:14:13.260 --> 00:14:16.463
how unlikely they private
thought the report would be.

00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:21.740
